  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Strong thunderstorms are possible Thursday amidst\nincreasingly warm and humid conditions.\n\nAny residual stratocumulus decks have dissipated with the loss\nof daytime heating. However, high clouds continue to stream in\nfrom the northwest which currently extend back toward Lake Erie.\nLow humidity air sticks around through tonight. Surface high \npressure will be nearly overhead, with light winds allowing low \ntemperatures to drop to the mid 50s to mid 60s. Patchy fog is \npossible, especially west of the Blue Ridge, although the\nmentioned passing high clouds may prevent full radiational \ncooling.\n\nHigh pressure will depart off the coast Wednesday with surface\nwinds turning southerly. Some afternoon gusts up to 20 mph are \npossible depending on the degree of vertical mixing. High \ntemperatures will tick up into the upper 80s to near 90, which \ncomes with an increase in dew points/humidity levels. It looks \nlike the atmosphere will remain capped most of the day, although\na pop up shower along the terrain could occur in the afternoon.\nMoisture return increases overnight, which could result in some\nscattered showers and storms. However, most of the 12Z guidance\nhas very limited to no coverage.\n\nMore notable forcing arrives Thursday as an upper level trough \nand surface cold front approach from the northwest. However, \nthese forcing features may be somewhat displaced from the local \narea during peak diurnal heating. The front likely lags to the \nnorthwest while the strongest forcing with the trough passes to \nthe north. A secondary shortwave may approach but not until the \nevening. Thus there remains some uncertainty in the coverage and\ntiming of any showers and thunderstorms. There is also some \nquestion on instability as there may be a fair amount of cloud \ncover around and perhaps some lingering morning showers. If \neverything were to come together though, moderate instability \ncombined with shear of 20-30 kt could result in some strong to \nlocally severe thunderstorms with damaging winds being the \nprimary threat. Before and storms arrive, it will be warm and \nhumid with highs in the mid 80s to near 90.\n\nThe cold front may bisect the area by Friday morning while the\nmid/upper level trough axis remains to the west. Some showers\nand thunderstorms will continue to be possible near and south of\nthe front, although the risk for strong thunderstorms is lower.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...After a brief cool down this weekend, hot\nweather will return next week.\n\nThe front may weaken or stall to the south this weekend while\nhigh pressure builds eastward from the Great Lakes to St.\nLawrence Valley. Overall, the high should be strong enough to\nshove most of the moisture to the south, although there is some\nlingering potential for showers across southern areas on\nSaturday. The coolest temperatures are also most likely\nSaturday, with warming conditions expected Sunday as the high\npushes off to the east. Upper level ridging also builds eastward\non Sunday. Combined with return flow around the high, hotter\nconditions are expected early next week with high temperatures \nrising back into the 90s by Monday for many locations. A \ndiurnally driven shower or thunderstorm is possible, but the \nridging should suppress most precipitation.",
